{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS’ Customer Carbon Footprint Tool now includes Scope 3 emissions data - AWS","context":"AWS has updated its Customer Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT) to include Scope 3 emissions data and Scope 1 natural gas and refrigerants. This expansion allows AWS customers to have a more comprehensive view of their cloud carbon footprint, covering all three industry-standard emission scopes as defined by the Greenhouse Gas Protocol. The update provides visibility into the lifecycle carbon impact of AWS usage, including emissions from manufacturing servers, powering AWS facilities, and transporting equipment to data centers. Historical data is available from January 2022, enabling organizations to track their progress and make informed decisions about their cloud strategy to meet sustainability goals. The updated CCFT data is accessible through the dashboard and AWS Billing and Cost Management Data Exports, allowing for easy integration into operational workflows, sustainability planning, and reporting processes. For more information, visit the AWS Billing and Cost Management console or read the updated methodology documentation and release notes","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/10/aws-customer-carbon-footprint-tool-scope-3-emissions-data/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Customer Carbon Footprint Tool now includes location-based emissions - AWS","context":"The AWS Customer Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT) and Data Exports now display emissions calculated using the location-based method (LBM) in addition to the previously available market-based method (MBM). The LBM reflects the average emissions intensity of the electricity grids where energy consumption occurs. This update allows users to view and analyze monthly carbon emissions that align more closely with their cloud usage and gain insights into the carbon intensity of AWS data centers' underlying electricity grids. CloudFront emissions estimates are also now included in the service breakdown. The document explains the differences between LBM and MBM in detail, and users can access their LBM emissions in the Customer Carbon Footprint Tool and Data Exports","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/06/customer-carbon-footprint-tool-location-based-emissions/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS reduces publishing time for Carbon Footprint Data to 21 days or Less - AWS","context":"AWS has significantly reduced the publishing time for carbon footprint data from up to three months to 21 days or less. This improvement provides users with more timely insights into their carbon emissions and resource usage. The Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT) dashboard now offers carbon footprint data with estimates published between the 15th and 21st of the month following usage. Additionally, the CCFT dashboard maintains 38 months of data, enabling users to view their carbon usage trends over an extended period. To access the carbon footprint data, users can navigate to their carbon emissions data through the AWS Billing and Cost Management console. For further information about CCFT, users can visit the CCFT capabilities and features page, review the user guides, and explore the CCFT webpage","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/12/aws-reduces-publishing-time-carbon-footprint-data/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-marketplace-metering","skill_description":"Deploys, queries, and debugs AWS Marketplace usage-based (PAYG) metering — the pipeline (ResolveCustomer, BatchMeterUsage, EventBridge via SAM) and querying/debugging metering records, statuses, failures, discrepancies. Covers Concurrent Agreements (CustomerAWSAccountId + LicenseArn), multi-product, CloudTrail Event History (90 days; never CloudTrail Lake), Seller Reports (Billed Revenue/Usage columns + CSV/SDDS parsing), existing-stack detection, metering region for BatchMeterUsage/MeterUsage (DisabledApiException), metered AMI/Container + IAM roles, where registration data lives (customer-profile table), legacy ProductCode + CustomerIdentifier, DuplicateRecord/CustomerNotSubscribed, bad LicenseArn. Also SCOPES adjacent asks — flat-rate/SaaS-Subscription \"do I still meter\", CCP included-units + overage, contract term/annual renewal, forecasting future bills/usage — with the right answer or a one-line redirect. Use for any BatchMeterUsage/ResolveCustomer or SaaS usage-billing question.\n\nServices: aws-marketplace, marketplace-metering\nTasks: deploy, integrate, query, debug, troubleshoot, analyze\nPersona: developer\nWorkload: saas\n*","skill_name":"aws-marketplace-metering"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-transform","skill_description":"Performs code upgrades, migrations, and transformations using the AWS Transform (ATX) CLI. Use when upgrading language versions, migrating AWS SDKs, migrating frameworks (Angular, Vue.js, Spring Boot, React), upgrading libraries, optimizing performance, migrating x86 to Graviton, analyzing codebases / generating documentation, or defining custom transformations with natural language. Runs locally on a few repositories or at scale across hundreds via AWS Batch/Fargate.\n\nServices: transform\nTasks: migrate, upgrade, refactor, analyze\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: ai, migration, modernization\n*","skill_name":"aws-transform"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify AWS Customer Carbon Footprint Tool methodology and model versioning behavior"}
